<h2>Frequently Asked Questions on Digital Strain</h2>
<h3>What is computer vision syndrome?</h3>
<p>Computer vision syndrome refers to eye problems caused by excessive screen time. Dry eyes, blurred vision, headaches, and neck pain that builds throughout the day are the most common signs.</p>
<h3>Do blue light glasses help with digital eye strain?</h3>
<p>Current research finds no meaningful difference in eye comfort between blue-light-filtering lenses and regular lenses. An outdated prescription is a more common cause of screen strain than blue light.</p>
<h3>How is a computer vision exam different from a regular eye exam?</h3>
<p>A regular eye exam checks your overall vision and eye health. A computer vision exam specifically assesses how your eyes function at screen distance. This includes focusing ability and eye coordination, which a standard checkup may not catch.</p>
<h3>How long does digital eye strain take to go away?</h3>
<p>For most people, symptoms ease within a few hours of stepping away from screens. If strain persists into the next morning or worsens each day, that is a sign that something beyond screen habits needs to be addressed.</p>
<h3>Can eye strain cause headaches?</h3>
<p>Yes. The muscle fatigue from holding focus at screen distance for hours creates tension that often shows up as a headache behind the eyes or at the temples. It is one of the most common complaints among remote workers and students.</p>
<h3>Does screen time permanently damage your eyes?</h3>
<p>Current evidence says no. Screen use does not cause permanent eye damage. What it does cause is real, cumulative discomfort that affects how well you function day-to-day, and that is worth addressing.</p>
